<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-transformations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Transformations and Linear Transformations</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-transformations">
    <title>Transformations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-transformation">
      <title>Transformation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A <term>transformation</term> is a function <m>T : \R^n \to \R^m</m>, sometimes
          written <m>\R^n \xrightarrow{T} \R^m</m>, and is called a
          <term>transformation from <m>\R^n</m> to <m>\R^m</m></term>. The sets
          <m>\R^n</m> and <m>\R^m</m> are known as the <term>domain</term> and
          <term>codomain</term> of <m>T</m>. If <m>m = n</m>, then we say <m>T</m> is a
          transformation of <m>\R^n</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <remark xml:id="rem-what-is-a-function">
      <title>What do we mean by a function?</title>
      <p>
        Informally, a function <m>T : \R^n \to \R^m</m> is a rule that assigns exactly one
        vector of <m>\R^m</m> to each vector of <m>\R^n</m>. We use the notation
        <m>T(\vec{x})</m> to mean the transformation <m>T</m> applied to the vector
        <m>\vec{x}</m>.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <p>
      Before an example whose output has four coordinates, picture the
      transformation <m>T:\R^3\to\R^2</m> given by
      <md>
        T(x,y,z)=(x+2y,2x+y),\qquad
        A=\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}.
      </md>
      We will compute with this same matrix in
      <xref ref="eg-transformation-by-multiplication" text="type-global"/>.
      <em>Predict:</em> if only the <m>z</m>-coordinate of the input changes,
      should the output move? What does the third column suggest?
    </p>
    <p xml:id="intro-transformation-geometry">
      <em>Explore the geometry.</em>
      The domain is three-dimensional and the codomain is two-dimensional.
      The standard basis arrows, their images, and the columns of <m>A</m>
      share labels and colors. A transparent unit cube maps to a parallelogram;
      its <m>z</m>-edges collapse to points. The captioned guide follows the
      basis images, a sample vector, and several inputs with one common image.
    </p>
    <interactive xml:id="int-transformation-geometry" iframe="demos-manim/transformation-geometry.html" width="100%" aspect="6:5">
      <shortdescription>Synchronized 3D input and 2D output views for T(x,y,z) = (x+2y,2x+y), with basis images, coordinate controls, and inputs differing only in z.</shortdescription>
      <static>
        <image source="images/transformation-geometry-static.svg" width="100%">
          <shortdescription>The three basis vectors map to (1,2), (2,1), and (0,0). The unit cube maps to the parallelogram with corners (0,0), (1,2), (3,3), and (2,1).</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
    <p>
      The basis images are <m>T(\vec{e}_1)=(1,2)</m>,
      <m>T(\vec{e}_2)=(2,1)</m>, and <m>T(\vec{e}_3)=(0,0)</m>.
      Consequently,
      <md>
        T(x,y,z)=x(1,2)+y(2,1)+z(0,0).
      </md>
      In particular, <m>T(1,-1,2)=(-1,1)</m>. Every vector of the form
      <m>(1,-1,z)</m> has this same image: the transformation discards the
      <m>z</m>-coordinate. The first two columns are independent, so inputs
      have the same output precisely when their <m>x</m>- and
      <m>y</m>-coordinates agree.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Which different inputs produce the same output? What information does this transformation lose?</em>
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-matrix-transformation">
      <title>Matrix Transformation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>T</m> acts by matrix multiplication of a matrix <m>A</m>, we call
          <m>T</m> a <term>matrix transformation</term>, and write
          <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>. The vector <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m> is
          called the <term>image</term> of <m>\vec{x}</m>, and the set of all images is
          called the <term>range</term> of <m>T</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-matrix-transformation">
      <title>A Matrix Transformation from <m>\R^3</m> to <m>\R^4</m></title>
      <p>
        Consider <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^4</m> defined by
        <md>
          T \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} x + y \\ y \\ y - z \\ -y + z \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
        For instance, <m>T</m> transforms
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} \in \R^3</m> to
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -2 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} \in \R^4</m>. Separating the
        three variables shows that <m>T</m> is a matrix transformation:
        <md>
          T(\vec{x})
          = x \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
          + y \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}
          + z \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          = \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
              0 \amp -1 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}}_{A}
          \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}}_{\vec{x}}
          = T_A(\vec{x}).
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-attainable-outputs">
      <title>Which Outputs Occur, and from Which Input?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T = T_A</m> be the transformation of
          <xref ref="eg-matrix-transformation" text="type-global" />, and let
          <m>\vec{b} = \begin{bmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \\ b_4 \end{bmatrix}</m> be a
          proposed output.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Determine exactly when <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> has a solution, and
                find every input <m>\vec{x}</m> when it does.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Show that your condition is sufficient as well as necessary.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The output has four entries. Do they record four independent pieces of
                information about the input?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a)</term> The equation <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{b}</m> is the linear system
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>. Row reduce its augmented matrix:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b_1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b_2 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp b_3 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp b_4
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow[r_2 + r_4]{-r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b_1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b_2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp b_3 - b_2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp b_2 + b_4
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{r_3 + r_4}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b_1 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b_2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp b_3 - b_2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp b_3 + b_4
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow[-r_2 + r_1]{-r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp b_1 - b_2 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b_2 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp b_2 - b_3 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp b_3 + b_4
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          The last row reads <m>0 = b_3 + b_4</m>, so the system is consistent exactly
          when <m>b_4 = -b_3</m>. In that case every column of <m>A</m> has a pivot,
          there is no free variable, and there is exactly one input:
          <md>
            \vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} b_1 - b_2 \\ b_2 \\ b_2 - b_3 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b)</term> The condition is necessary because of the last row. It is
          sufficient because, when <m>b_4 = -b_3</m>, the input found in (a) really
          does produce <m>\vec{b}</m>:
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} b_1 - b_2 \\ b_2 \\ b_2 - b_3 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix}
                (b_1 - b_2) + b_2 \\ b_2 \\ b_2 - (b_2 - b_3) \\ -b_2 + (b_2 - b_3)
              \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} b_1 \\ b_2 \\ b_3 \\ -b_3 \end{bmatrix}
            = \vec{b}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c)</term> No. The fourth entry of every output is the negative of the
          third, since <m>-y + z = -(y - z)</m>, so it repeats what the third entry
          already says. The range of <m>T</m> is the set of vectors of <m>\R^4</m> with
          <m>b_3 + b_4 = 0</m>, not all of <m>\R^4</m>. For example,
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -2 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m> is an output, and the
          formula in (a) returns its input
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>, whereas
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is not an output of any
          input.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two separate facts appeared here: every output that occurs comes from only one
          input, and not every vector of the codomain occurs.
          <xref ref="sec-injections-surjections" text="type-global" /> gives each of them
          a name.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="def-equality-of-transformations">
      <title>Equality of Transformations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>S : \R^n \to \R^m</m> and <m>T : \R^n \to \R^m</m> are
          transformations. Then <m>S = T</m> if and only if <m>S(\vec{x}) = T(\vec{x})</m>
          for every <m>\vec{x} \in \R^n</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-transformation-by-multiplication">
      <title>Transformation by Matrix Multiplication</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the matrix
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          By matrix multiplication, <m>A</m> transforms vectors in <m>\R^3</m> into
          vectors in <m>\R^2</m>. Describe the effect on a general vector, and compute the
          image of <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Transforming a general vector by <m>A</m> looks like this:
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}_{2 \times 3}
            \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}_{3 \times 1}
            = \begin{bmatrix} x + 2y \\ 2x + y \end{bmatrix}_{2 \times 1}.
          </md>
          For example,
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-transformation-schematic">
      <caption>
        A transformation <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^2</m> sends a vector <m>\vec{x}</m> in
        3-space to its image <m>A\vec{x}</m> in the plane.
      </caption>
      <image width="78%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\small}]
            \begin{scope}[x={(-0.42cm,-0.30cm)}, y={(0.85cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,0.85cm)}]
              \draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (0,0,0) -- (2.6,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
              \draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.8,0) node[right] {$y$};
              \draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.6) node[above] {$z$};
              \draw[bookwine, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2.2mm]}] (0,0,0) -- (1.6,1.0,1.7)
                node[above right, black] {$\vec{x}$};
            \end{scope}
            \begin{scope}[xshift=6.4cm]
              \draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (-0.4,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[-{Stealth[length=2mm]}] (0,-0.9) -- (0,2.6) node[above] {$y$};
              \draw[bookolive, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2.2mm]}] (0,0) -- (1.7,1.5)
                node[above right, black] {$A\vec{x}$};
            \end{scope}
            \draw[bookolive, dashed, -{Stealth[length=2.4mm]}] (1.6,2.6)
              to[out=35, in=145] node[midway, above] {$T:\R^3 \to \R^2$} (6.0,2.2);
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-recovering-the-input">
      <title>Recovering the Input from the Output</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T(x, y, z) = (x + 2y,\, 2x + y)</m> be the transformation of
          <xref ref="eg-transformation-by-multiplication" text="type-global" />, and
          suppose that only the output
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} p \\ q \end{bmatrix}</m> is observed.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Find every input whose output is
                <m>\begin{bmatrix} p \\ q \end{bmatrix}</m>. Which coordinate of the
                input cannot be recovered, and which are determined?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                A second instrument records <m>m = x - y + z</m>. Do the three numbers
                <m>p</m>, <m>q</m>, <m>m</m> determine the input? Give formulas for it
                and verify them.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Explain why recording <m>m = x - y</m> instead would not repair the loss
                of information.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a)</term> Row reduce the augmented matrix of
          <m>A\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} p \\ q \end{bmatrix}</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp p \\
                2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp q
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp p \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp q - 2p
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-\frac{1}{3} r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp p \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{2p - q}{3}
              \end{array} \right]
              \xrightarrow{-2r_2 + r_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \frac{2q - p}{3} \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp \frac{2p - q}{3}
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          The third column has no pivot, so <m>z = t</m> is free:
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{2q - p}{3} \\ \frac{2p - q}{3} \\ t \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad t \in \R.
          </md>
          The output determines <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, and it says nothing about
          <m>z</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b)</term> Yes. The three readings give three equations:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp p \\
                2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp q \\
                1 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp m
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow[-r_1 + r_3]{-2r_1 + r_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp p \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp q - 2p \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 1 \amp m - p
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-r_2 + r_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
                1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp p \\
                0 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp q - 2p \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp m + p - q
              \end{array} \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Now every column has a pivot, so the input is unique:
          <md>
            x = \frac{2q - p}{3}, \qquad y = \frac{2p - q}{3}, \qquad z = m + p - q.
          </md>
          To verify the new formula, note that <m>x - y = q - p</m>, so
          <m>x - y + z = (q - p) + (m + p - q) = m</m>. For instance, the input
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m> gives <m>p = -1</m>,
          <m>q = 1</m>, <m>m = 4</m>, and the formulas return
          <m>x = 1</m>, <m>y = -1</m>, <m>z = 2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c)</term> With <m>m = x - y</m> the third row of the augmented matrix is
          <m>\left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp m \end{array} \right]</m>,
          and the same row operations end with
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp p \\
              0 \amp -3 \amp 0 \amp q - 2p \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp m + p - q
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
          The third column still has no pivot, so <m>z</m> remains free. This reading
          can be computed from the other two, because
          <m>x - y = (2x + y) - (x + 2y) = q - p</m>; it repeats what is already known. A
          new reading helps only if it involves <m>z</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-linear-transformations">
    <title>Linear Transformations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-linear-transformation">
      <title>Linear Transformation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A transformation <m>T : \R^n \to \R^m</m> is a
          <term>linear transformation</term> if it satisfies the following two properties
          for all <m>\vec{x}, \vec{y} \in \R^n</m> and all scalars <m>a \in \R</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>T(\vec{x} + \vec{y}) = T(\vec{x}) + T(\vec{y})</m>
                (preservation of addition).
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>T(a\vec{x}) = aT(\vec{x})</m>
                (preservation of scalar multiplication).
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      A first example comes from geometry. Let <m>R_\theta : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> be the
      transformation that rotates every vector counterclockwise about the origin through
      the angle <m>\theta</m>. The animation in
      <xref ref="fig-rotation-is-linear" text="type-global" /> checks the two properties of
      <xref ref="def-linear-transformation" text="type-global" /> for <m>R_\theta</m>,
      using only what a rotation does to a picture. Rotations return in
      <xref ref="sec-special-transformations" text="type-global" />, where the matrix of
      <m>R_\theta</m> is found.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-rotation-is-linear">
      <caption>
        A rotation is a linear transformation. Rotating the plane carries the
        parallelogram on <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> to the parallelogram on
        <m>R_\theta(\vec{u})</m> and <m>R_\theta(\vec{v})</m>, so the rotated diagonal
        <m>R_\theta(\vec{u} + \vec{v})</m> is the diagonal
        <m>R_\theta(\vec{u}) + R_\theta(\vec{v})</m> of the new parallelogram. A rotation
        also carries a line through the origin to a line through the origin and does not
        change lengths, so <m>R_\theta(a\vec{u}) = aR_\theta(\vec{u})</m> for every
        scalar <m>a</m>.
      </caption>
      <video xml:id="video-rotation-is-linear"
             source="videos/rotation_is_linear_manim.mp4"
             preview="videos/previews/rotation_is_linear_manim.jpg"
             width="90%" aspect="16:9"/>
    </figure>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-linear-transformation-properties">
      <title>Properties of Linear Transformations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^n \to \R^m</m> be a linear transformation, and let
          <m>\vec{x} \in \R^n</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>T(0\vec{x}) = 0T(\vec{x})</m>, implying <m>T(\vec{0}) = \vec{0}</m>, so
                <m>T</m> preserves the zero vector.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>T((-1)\vec{x}) = (-1)T(\vec{x})</m>, implying
                <m>T(-\vec{x}) = -T(\vec{x})</m>, so <m>T</m> preserves the negative of a
                vector.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Suppose <m>\vec{x}_1, \vec{x}_2, \ldots, \vec{x}_k</m> are vectors in
                <m>\R^n</m> and
                <m>\vec{y} = a_1\vec{x}_1 + a_2\vec{x}_2 + \cdots + a_k\vec{x}_k</m>
                for some <m>a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_k \in \R</m>. Then
                <md>
                  <mrow>
                    T(\vec{y}) \amp = T(a_1\vec{x}_1 + a_2\vec{x}_2 + \cdots + a_k\vec{x}_k)
                  </mrow>
                  <mrow>
                    \amp = a_1 T(\vec{x}_1) + a_2 T(\vec{x}_2) + \cdots + a_k T(\vec{x}_k),
                  </mrow>
                </md>
                i.e., <m>T</m> preserves linear combinations.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Each part uses only the two properties in
          <xref ref="def-linear-transformation" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Preservation of scalar multiplication with the scalar <m>0</m> gives
                <m>T(0\vec{x}) = 0T(\vec{x})</m>. On the left, <m>0\vec{x}</m> is the zero
                vector of <m>\R^n</m>. On the right, <m>0</m> times any vector of
                <m>\R^m</m> is the zero vector of <m>\R^m</m>. Hence
                <m>T(\vec{0}) = \vec{0}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Preservation of scalar multiplication with the scalar <m>-1</m> gives
                <m>T((-1)\vec{x}) = (-1)T(\vec{x})</m>. Since
                <m>(-1)\vec{x} = -\vec{x}</m> and
                <m>(-1)T(\vec{x}) = -T(\vec{x})</m>, this says
                <m>T(-\vec{x}) = -T(\vec{x})</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                For two vectors, use preservation of addition and then preservation of
                scalar multiplication:
                <md>
                  T(a_1\vec{x}_1 + a_2\vec{x}_2)
                  = T(a_1\vec{x}_1) + T(a_2\vec{x}_2)
                  = a_1 T(\vec{x}_1) + a_2 T(\vec{x}_2).
                </md>
                For <m>k</m> vectors, split off the last term in the same way,
                <md>
                  T\bigl( (a_1\vec{x}_1 + \cdots + a_{k-1}\vec{x}_{k-1}) + a_k\vec{x}_k \bigr)
                  = T(a_1\vec{x}_1 + \cdots + a_{k-1}\vec{x}_{k-1}) + a_k T(\vec{x}_k),
                </md>
                and repeat the step on the shorter sum that remains. After
                <m>k - 1</m> steps every term has been split off, which gives
                <md>
                  T(a_1\vec{x}_1 + a_2\vec{x}_2 + \cdots + a_k\vec{x}_k)
                  = a_1 T(\vec{x}_1) + a_2 T(\vec{x}_2) + \cdots + a_k T(\vec{x}_k).
                </md>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="eg-transformation-from-values">
      <title>Determining a Transformation from Two Values</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^3 \to \R^4</m> be a linear transformation such that
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 4 \\ 0 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}
            \qquad \text{and} \qquad
            T \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \\ -1 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Find <m>T \begin{bmatrix} -7 \\ 3 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First, we need to see if
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} -7 \\ 3 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix}</m> can be written as a linear
          combination of <m>\vec{x}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
          <m>\vec{x}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Solving
          <m>a_1 \vec{x}_1 + a_2 \vec{x}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -7 \\ 3 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix}</m>:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 4 \amp -7 \\ 3 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\ 1 \amp 5 \amp -9
            \end{array} \right]
            \longrightarrow
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 4 \amp -7 \\ 0 \amp -12 \amp 24 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -2
            \end{array} \right]
            \longrightarrow
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right],
          </md>
          so <m>a_1 = 1</m> and <m>a_2 = -2</m>; that is,
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} -7 \\ 3 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix}
            = (1) \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            - 2 \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Then we use property (3) of
          <xref ref="thm-linear-transformation-properties" text="type-global" />:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              T \begin{bmatrix} -7 \\ 3 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix}
              \amp = (1) \, T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
              - 2 \, T \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 0 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 4 \\ 0 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}
              - 2 \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 5 \\ -1 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}
              = \begin{bmatrix} -4 \\ -6 \\ 2 \\ -12 \end{bmatrix}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-matrix-transformations-are-linear">
    <title>Matrix Transformations Are Linear</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-matrix-transformations-linear">
      <title>Every Matrix Transformation Is a Linear Transformation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Every matrix transformation is a linear transformation.</p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Consider the matrix transformation <m>T_A(\vec{x}) = A\vec{x}</m>. We will show
          that such a transformation is in fact linear, i.e., it preserves addition and
          scalar multiplication.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(1) Preservation of addition.</term>
          Let <m>A</m> be <m>m \times n</m> with columns
          <m>\vec{A}_1, \ldots, \vec{A}_n</m>, and let
          <m>\vec{x}, \vec{y} \in \R^n</m>. Then
          <md>
            <mrow>
              T_A(\vec{x} + \vec{y}) \amp = A(\vec{x} + \vec{y})
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = (x_1 + y_1)\vec{A}_1 + (x_2 + y_2)\vec{A}_2 + \cdots + (x_n + y_n)\vec{A}_n
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \left( x_1\vec{A}_1 + \cdots + x_n\vec{A}_n \right)
                   + \left( y_1\vec{A}_1 + \cdots + y_n\vec{A}_n \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = A\vec{x} + A\vec{y} = T_A(\vec{x}) + T_A(\vec{y}).
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(2) Preservation of scalar multiplication.</term>
          Let <m>c \in \R</m> and <m>\vec{x} \in \R^n</m>. Then
          <md>
            <mrow>
              T_A(c\vec{x}) \amp = A(c\vec{x})
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = (cx_1)\vec{A}_1 + (cx_2)\vec{A}_2 + \cdots + (cx_n)\vec{A}_n
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = c \left( x_1\vec{A}_1 + x_2\vec{A}_2 + \cdots + x_n\vec{A}_n \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = c \left( A\vec{x} \right) = c \, T_A(\vec{x}).
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="eg-not-a-matrix-transformation">
      <title>Transformations That Are Not Matrix Transformations</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Can you come up with an example of a transformation that is NOT a matrix
          transformation? Explain why it is not a matrix transformation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          As an example, consider <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> defined by
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = \vec{x} + \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>. You can
          easily check that <m>T</m> does not preserve <m>\vec{0}</m>, since
          <m>T(\vec{0}) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \neq \vec{0}</m>, and hence
          it is not a linear transformation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Also, we cannot find a matrix <m>A</m> such that for all
          <m>\vec{x} \in \R^2</m> we have
          <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{x} + \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Again consider
          <m>\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>. Then
          <m>\vec{0} = \vec{0} + \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, which is
          impossible.
        </p>
        <p>
          As another example, consider <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> defined by
          <m>T(\vec{x}) = \begin{bmatrix} x^2 \\ y \end{bmatrix}</m>. If such an <m>A</m>
          existed, then
          <md>
            A\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
            = x \begin{bmatrix} a \\ c \end{bmatrix} + y \begin{bmatrix} b \\ d \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} x^2 \\ y \end{bmatrix}
          </md>
          would require <m>ax + by = x^2</m> for all <m>x, y</m>, which is not possible.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-incomplete-linearity-test">
      <title>Passing a Few Checks Is Not Enough</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For real numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, define <m>F : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> by
          <md>
            F \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} x + a\,y(y - 1) + b \\ y \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Determine exactly which <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> make <m>F</m> a linear
                transformation.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Take <m>b = 0</m> and <m>a \neq 0</m>. Show that
                <m>F(\vec{0}) = \vec{0}</m>, <m>F(\vec{e}_1) = \vec{e}_1</m> and
                <m>F(\vec{e}_2) = \vec{e}_2</m>, and that <m>F</m> is still not linear.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                A matrix is determined by its columns, and the columns are the images of
                <m>\vec{e}_1</m> and <m>\vec{e}_2</m>. Why does this not show that the
                <m>F</m> of part (b) is the transformation
                <m>\vec{x} \mapsto \vec{x}</m>?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(a)</term> A linear transformation preserves the zero vector
          (<xref ref="thm-linear-transformation-properties" text="type-global" />), and
          <m>F(\vec{0}) = \begin{bmatrix} b \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, so <m>b = 0</m> is
          necessary. With <m>b = 0</m>,
          <md>
            F \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            F \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 2a \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Preservation of scalar multiplication requires
          <m>F \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
          = 2\,F \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>, so <m>a = 0</m> is necessary too.
          Conversely, when <m>a = b = 0</m> we have <m>F(\vec{x}) = \vec{x}</m>, the
          matrix transformation of
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, which is linear by
          <xref ref="thm-matrix-transformations-linear" text="type-global" />. Hence
          <m>F</m> is linear exactly when <m>a = b = 0</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(b)</term> The term <m>a\,y(y - 1)</m> vanishes when <m>y = 0</m> and when
          <m>y = 1</m>, so
          <md>
            F \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            F \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            F \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Yet <m>F</m> is not linear, by part (a):
          <m>F(2\vec{e}_2) = \begin{bmatrix} 2a \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
          \neq 2F(\vec{e}_2)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(c)</term> The statement about columns is a statement about linear
          transformations. If <m>T</m> is known to be linear, then
          <m>T \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix} = xT(\vec{e}_1) + yT(\vec{e}_2)</m>
          by <xref ref="thm-linear-transformation-properties" text="type-global" />, so
          the two basis images determine <m>T</m>. For a function that is not known to be
          linear, that step is not available. The matrix with columns
          <m>F(\vec{e}_1)</m> and <m>F(\vec{e}_2)</m> is
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and its
          transformation sends <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m> to itself,
          while <m>F</m> sends it to <m>\begin{bmatrix} 2a \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          One failed instance, as in
          <xref ref="eg-not-a-matrix-transformation" text="type-global" />, is enough to
          show that a transformation is not linear. Passing the test at a few chosen
          vectors shows nothing: the two properties must hold for all vectors and all
          scalars.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
